<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Eugène Delacroix</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1798">1798</a>–<a href="/years/1863">1863</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Ferdinand Victor Eugène Delacroix, Delacroix</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Eugène Delacroix was a French Romantic artist regarded as the leader of the French Romantic school. Against the Neoclassical perfectionism of his rival Ingres, he took Rubens and the painters of the Venetian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> as models, stressing colour and movement over clarity of outline. His Barque of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dante-alighieri">Dante</a> was accepted by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> Salon of 1822, and he went on to paint the Chios Massacre and The Death of Sardanapalus. Liberty Leading the People of 1830 was bought by the French government but removed from public view by 1832. His expressive brushstrokes and study of colour shaped the work of the Impressionists.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/eugene-delacroix.jpg" alt="Eugène Delacroix" width="330" height="470" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nadar · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Félix_Nadar_1820-1910_portraits_Eugène_Delacroix_restored.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Others prepared the way: the Romantic colourist <b>Eugène Delacroix</b>, J. M. W. Turner, the painters of the Barbizon school such as Théodore Rousseau, and Johan Barthold Jongkind, Jean-Baptiste-Camille Corot and Eugène Boudin, who painted from nature in a direct, spontaneous style and befriended the younger artists.</blockquote><a href="/movement/impressionism">Impressionism · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>That year <b>Eugène Delacroix</b> painted Liberty Leading the People, after the July Revolution, and Hector Berlioz’s Symphonie fantastique was first performed.</blockquote><a href="/movement/romanticism">Romanticism · Course of Events</a></li></ul></section>
